Expanding $R\sin(x-\alpha)$ and comparing coefficients gives $R\cos\alpha=2$ and $R\sin\alpha=2$. Hence $R=2\sqrt2$ and $\alpha=\frac\pi4$.
$\begin{align*}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}2\sin x-2\cos x\\&=2\sqrt2\sin\left(x-\frac\pi4\right)\end{align*}$
The amplitude is $2\sqrt2$ and the period is $2\pi$. The line $y=2$ intersects the curve at $x=\frac\pi2$ and $x=\pi$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[->,black] (-0.2,0)--(6.8,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3.25)--(0,3.45) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[black,domain=0:6.283185,samples=120] plot (\x,{2*sin(\x r)-2*cos(\x r)});
\draw[blue!0!black,dashed] (0,2)--(6.283185,2);
\path (0,2) node[left]{$2$};
\path (0,-2) node[left]{$-2$};
\path (2.356194,2.828427) node[above]{$2\sqrt2$};
\path (5.497787,-2.828427) node[below]{$-2\sqrt2$};
\draw[dashed,black] (1.570796,0)--(1.570796,2);
\draw[dashed,black] (3.141593,0)--(3.141593,2);
\foreach \p/\lab in {1.570796/{\frac\pi2},3.141593/{\pi},4.712389/{\frac{3\pi}2},6.283185/{2\pi}} {
\draw[black] (\p,-0.08)--(\p,0.08);
\path (\p,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont]{$\lab$};
}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The curve lies above $y=2$ between the intersections, so $\frac\pi2<x<\pi$. The strict inequality excludes both boundary points.
